## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/contentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackContext`): Attachment point for Live Preview: this is the single React context that holds all CMS content and feeds every Contentstack component via useContent(). Live Preview data from Contentstack's real-time SDK must be injected here — the context value is what all components read from, so updating it with live preview payload instantly propagates to every consumer.
  - **Minimal fix:** Extend IContentstackContext to carry live preview state (e.g., a livePreviewData field or a content hash), and update the context provider (wherever it wraps the app) to subscribe to Contentstack Live Preview events and merge the incoming draft content into the existing context value so that useContent→getContentByKey returns the preview data instead of the published data. The existing useContent hook (src/hooks/useContent.ts) and getValue util (src/utils/getValue) need no changes — they already traverse whatever object the context holds.

## Reviewer Feedback — ADDRESS THESE (a prior code review requested changes)
The team-lead reviewer examined your previous attempt and requested the changes below. This is the highest priority: make the SMALLEST edits that resolve each point. If a point says the change is over-engineered or a more concise change/existing helper would do, REMOVE the excess and use the minimal approach — do not add more code.
- [blocker] The `onEntryChange` callback does `setValue((prev) => ({ ...prev }))` — a shallow copy of the same state with NO new data merged. The prescribed fix requires merging incoming draft content so that `useContent→getContentByKey` returns preview data instead of published data. This callback is a no-op: it triggers a re-render with identical values. The `livePreviewData` field added to `IContentstackContext` is never populated by the SDK callback, and `getContentByKey` (via `getValue`) traverses paths like `page.0.title` — it has no knowledge of `livePreviewData` and no merge logic overlays preview data onto published fields. (src/context/contentstackContext.tsx:39)
  - Suggested fix: The `onEntryChange` callback must actually merge the incoming entry data into the context. Since the SDK's `onEntryChange` callback signature takes no arguments (confirmed via resolve_package_symbol: `OnEntryChangeCallback` is `() => void`), the SDK presumably patches the response objects in place or provides data through another mechanism. Investigate how the Contentstack Live Preview SDK delivers updated entry data (e.g., via `ContentstackLivePreview.getData()`, or by intercepting API responses), and in the callback, fetch the updated entry and merge it into the context value so that existing path-based lookups via `getValue` return the preview data. For example: `setValue((prev) => ({ ...prev, page: [updatedEntry] }))` or deep-merge the changed entry into the relevant top-level field.
- [blocker] `ContentstackProvider` is dead code — codegraph_query for `ContentstackProvider` returned zero callers. The existing context consumers (`useContent`, `useDirectory`, `usePageBaseURLs`, `MobileNav`, `TopBar`, `PageHead`, etc.) all call `useContext(ContentstackContext)` directly and are NOT wrapped in `ContentstackProvider`. Without wrapping the app tree in this provider, the `onEntryChange` subscription in its `useEffect` never runs, and no live preview data ever reaches consumers. The diff adds a provider but does not integrate it anywhere in the app. (src/context/contentstackContext.tsx:29)
  - Suggested fix: Either (a) wire `ContentstackProvider` into the app's root layout (e.g., `_app.tsx` or equivalent Next.js wrapper) so it wraps all consumers, passing the existing context data as `initialValue`; OR (b) add the `onEntryChange` subscription to wherever the context is currently populated (the existing provider/wrapper that feeds `ContentstackContext`), avoiding the new provider entirely. Option (b) is more concise and follows the prescribed minimal fix more closely.
- [blocker] The `livePreviewData` field on `IContentstackContext` is never populated by any code path and is never read by `getContentByKey`/`getValue`. The prescribed fix says 'useContent→getContentByKey returns the preview data instead of the published data' — but `getValue(content, path, defaultValue)` traverses the context object by dot-path (e.g., `page.0.title`). Adding a separate `livePreviewData` map does not cause `getValue` to return preview data for existing paths. The field is dead weight that gives a false impression of compliance. (src/context/contentstackContext.tsx:19)
  - Suggested fix: Remove `livePreviewData` from the interface. Instead, merge the incoming preview entry data directly into the existing top-level fields (`page`, `header`, `footer`, etc.) so that `getValue` naturally returns the updated values when traversing the same paths the components already use.
- [blocker] CONCISION & REUSE: The diff adds 35+ lines of new code (a provider component, a new `useContentstack` hook, a new `IContentstackProviderProps` interface) that are all unused dead code. A more concise change: add only the `onEntryChange` subscription + data-merge logic to the existing location where `ContentstackContext` is populated (the existing provider or page wrapper that sets the context value). This would be ~10 lines and would actually connect to the existing consumer tree. The new `useContentstack` hook (lines 51-53) duplicates what `useContent` already does (`useContext(ContentstackContext)`) and is never imported anywhere. (src/context/contentstackContext.tsx:29)
  - Suggested fix: Remove `ContentstackProvider`, `IContentstackProviderProps`, and `useContentstack`. Instead, find the existing code that populates `ContentstackContext` (the component or page that provides the context value) and add the `onEntryChange` subscription there, merging incoming entry data into the context state.
- [major] Tests pass by mutating `initialValue` directly before firing the callback, relying on the fact that `setValue((prev) => ({ ...prev }))` preserves nested object references. This tests that React re-renders on a state reference change, NOT that the provider receives and merges live preview data from the SDK. In a real scenario, the SDK would deliver new entry data through its own mechanism — the tests don't verify that the provider correctly processes and merges that data. The test at line 73 (`initialValue.livePreviewData!.entryUid1 = { title: 'Updated' }`) mutates the source object, which is an anti-pattern that masks the fact that the provider never independently receives data. (src/context/contentstackContext.spec.ts:73)
  - Suggested fix: Mock the SDK to deliver updated entry data through whatever mechanism the real SDK uses (e.g., mock a `getData()` return value or intercept the API response). Then verify the provider merges that data into context so `getContentByKey` returns the updated value — without pre-mutating the initial state.

## Lessons From Prior Runs (persisted — a previous attempt at this or a similar story already hit these problems)
Any public method returning Promise<void> that wraps a Node.js EventEmitter callback (e.g. server.listen, child_process.spawn, stream.pipe) MUST:
1. Pass BOTH resolve AND reject to the Promise executor: `new Promise((resolve, reject) => {`
2. Wire the error event to reject: `emitter.on('error', reject)` — EADDRINUSE, EACCES, and other async errors are emitted on the EventEmitter, NOT thrown synchronously. Without `server.on('error', reject)`, a port-collision hangs the Promise forever. 3. Validate inputs in the constructor BEFORE the Promise is created. TypeScript types are compile-time only; at runtime port=NaN/Infinity/undefined bypasses them. Use `Number.isInteger(port) && port >= 0 && port <= 65535` and throw RangeError if invalid. VERIFIABLE: `grep -n 'reject' src/index.ts` must return at least two hits — one in the executor signature `(resolve, reject)` and one in `.on('error', reject)`. MINIMAL CHANGE & REUSE. Make the smallest change that satisfies the requirement — fewer lines of code is always better. Before writing any new function or helper, search the existing codebase for one that already does the job (use the CodeGraph `helpers` tool when it is offered in your prompt) and reuse it by importing it; do NOT duplicate existing logic or introduce new abstractions/fields when a concise edit to existing code suffices. Acceptance criteria describe the behavior to satisfy — they are not a mandate to add new code paths. [Self-Heal] Always patch only the broken line range on a repeated syntax-error retry -- never regenerate the whole file, since a full rewrite tends to reproduce the same corruption elsewhere.
When working with Contentstack context, use the ContentstackLivePreview and ContentstackManagement patterns already established in src/context/contentstackContext.tsx — do not introduce new SDK initialization patterns.
When creating or modifying TypeScript interfaces in src/interface/content/, ensure they are compatible with the existing CommonJS module system — use `export interface` not `export default interface`.
For Contentstack content components, follow the naming convention already in the codebase: PascalCase for component directories and camelCase for mock files.

## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a content entry's published value is changed in the CMS live preview session, the page immediately reflects the updated value in the area of the page that displays that entry's content.
- When a content entry's published value is changed in the CMS live preview session, areas of the page displaying content from other entries remain unchanged.
- When a content entry's published value is changed in the CMS live preview session, the page's navigation bar immediately reflects the updated value.
- When the page is viewed outside a CMS live preview session, all content on the page displays the current published values.
- If the live preview session disconnects or encounters an error, the page continues to display the content values that were last shown.

